The helper checks one candidate divisor, and the recursive function keeps half of the range. A valid middle value is saved as a possible answer, while an invalid one means the divisor must be larger.

This version is a little less common than a loop, but the recursion depth is only logarithmic.

```go
func smallestDivisor(nums []int, limit int) int {
	maxValue := 0
	for _, value := range nums {
		if value > maxValue {
			maxValue = value
		}
	}

	var works = func(divisor int) bool {
		total := int64(0)
		for _, value := range nums {
			total += int64((value + divisor - 1) / divisor)
			if total > int64(limit) {
				return false
			}
		}
		return true
	}

	var search func(int, int) int
	search = func(left, right int) int {
		if left == right {
			return left
		}

		middle := left + (right-left)/2
		if works(middle) {
			return search(left, middle)
		}
		return search(middle+1, right)
	}

	return search(1, maxValue)
}
```

For a single value such as `nums = [19]` and `limit = 5`, divisors `1`, `2`, and `3` fail, while `4` gives `ceil(19 / 4) = 5`. The recursive search returns `4`.

Time complexity: `O(n log M)`.
Space complexity: `O(log M)` because of the recursive calls, where `M` is `max(nums)`.

The main search is standard, but the check stops as soon as the sum passes the limit. Once the total is already too large, the remaining values cannot make this divisor valid, so there is no need to scan them.

This is especially helpful when small divisors fail quickly. The important edge cases, such as an answer of `1` or an answer equal to the largest element, are covered by the initial bounds.

```java
class Solution {
    public int smallestDivisor(int[] nums, int limit) {
        int largest = 0;
        for (int value : nums) {
            largest = Math.max(largest, value);
        }

        int left = 1;
        int right = largest;
        int answer = largest;

        while (left <= right) {
            int divisor = left + (right - left) / 2;
            long total = 0;

            for (int value : nums) {
                total += (value + divisor - 1L) / divisor;
                if (total > limit) {
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (total <= limit) {
                answer = divisor;
                right = divisor - 1;
            } else {
                left = divisor + 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Dry run for `[1, 2, 5, 9]` with limit `6`: divisor `5` produces total `5`, so it is valid. The search then tests smaller values, and divisor `4` produces `7`, so the final answer remains `5`.

Time complexity: `O(n log M)` in the worst case, with possible early stopping in practice.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is not modified.

The sum gets smaller or stays the same when the divisor gets larger. That gives a simple yes or no pattern: small divisors fail, and after some point all divisors work. Binary search finds the first working divisor.

The range from `1` to the largest array value is enough. A divisor equal to the largest value makes every term equal to `1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int smallestDivisor(int[] nums, int limit) {
        int high = 0;
        for (int value : nums) {
            high = Math.max(high, value);
        }

        int low = 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int divisor = low + (high - low) / 2;
            long total = 0;

            for (int value : nums) {
                total += (value + divisor - 1L) / divisor;
            }

            if (total <= limit) {
                high = divisor;
            } else {
                low = divisor + 1;
            }
        }

        return low;
    }
}
```

For `nums = [1, 2, 5, 9]` and `limit = 6`, a divisor of `5` gives `1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5`, so the search keeps it and checks smaller values. Divisor `4` gives `7`, so it is rejected.

Time complexity: `O(n log M)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, not counting the input array.

The `long` total prevents the running sum from overflowing.

Kept this one very direct. Try divisors from `1` upward and stop at the first one whose rounded-up sum fits the limit. Ceiling division is done with integer math, so there is no floating point involved.

This is easy to follow, but it is slower than binary search when the largest number is big.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int smallestDivisor(vector<int> &nums, int limit) {
        int largest = 0;
        for (int value : nums) {
            largest = max(largest, value);
        }

        for (int divisor = 1; divisor <= largest; divisor++) {
            long long total = 0;
            for (int value : nums) {
                total += (value + divisor - 1) / divisor;
            }

            if (total <= limit) {
                return divisor;
            }
        }

        return largest;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * M)`, where `M` is the largest value in `nums`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The input array is not changed.

